## `tiredboffin/fffw` — https://github.com/tiredboffin/fffw

- **MIT, Copyright (c) 2024 tiredboffin@gmail.com.** Full text in
  `LICENSES/fffw-MIT.txt`.
- Cited as the published state of the art for Fujifilm research tooling:
  `ffun` (not published, "most modules don't even build yet") and `ff80` (a JIG
  tool requiring the camera). Neither is included or used at run time. Its
  per-body headers (`ffun/ports/*/task.h`, `ui_setp.h`) informed the
  cross-model reasoning recorded in `docs/PORTING.md`.

## `johnborillo/xt3-cfw` — https://github.com/johnborillo/xt3-cfw

- **MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 the xt3-cfw contributors.** Full text in
  `LICENSES/xt3-cfw-MIT.txt`.
- `evidence/hook-comparison-*.md` and `evidence/function-candidates.json`
  compare its 371 guarded X-T3 hook sites against the X-Pro3, X-T4 and X100V
  images. The recipe itself is not redistributed; the comparison is.
- **`recipes/fast-loader-xt3.json.xz` is redistributed verbatim**, from its
  `firmware/recipes/fast-loader.json.xz` at commit `1ac5136`. It is the seven
  `own` operations — 3 722 bytes — that make up its fast loader. They are its
  own code, not Fujifilm's, which is why they can be republished here; the
  `tools/build_xpro3_fast_loader.py` step that reuses them verifies first that
  every patch site is byte-identical between the X-T3 and X-Pro3 loaders.

## `fujihack/fujihack` — https://github.com/fujihack/fujihack

- **GPL-3.0.** Cited only; no code taken. Referenced in `docs/` for the
  service-mode (PTP) channel and the brick-recovery procedures.

## How to verify the citations

Every hash, address and count in `docs/` is reproducible from the named source
with the tools in `tools/`, except where a document explicitly says a value is
declared virtual rather than measured. Where the two disagree, the measurement
wins and `docs/CORRECTIONS-2026-11-27.md` records the correction.
